<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<div class="callout"><h2 id="glance" style="margin-top:0">At a glance</h2><ol class="stop-steps"><li><strong>Renting or no drilling:</strong> a <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorway bar</a> in a frame that can support it.</li><li><strong>Own a wall with studs:</strong> a <a href="/wall-mounted-bars/">wall-mounted bar</a>, the most stable fixed option.</li><li><strong>Open joists or room to spare overhead:</strong> a ceiling-mounted bar for clearance on every side.</li><li><strong>No wall, frame or joists to use:</strong> a freestanding power tower.</li><li><strong>Every type:</strong> a bar about 28 to 32 mm across and a rated load well above your bodyweight, 300 pounds or more for bodyweight hangs.</li></ol><p>Read the exact product's rated load and mounting instructions before ordering.</p></div><p class="evidence-note"><strong>Affiliate disclosure:</strong> As an Amazon Associate, DeadHangs.com earns from qualifying purchases. Links marked affiliate link open Amazon search results, not single products. DeadHangs.com has not physically tested any of this equipment, so this page compares mounting types, fit checks and manufacturer specifications. See <a href="/equipment-reviews/">how DeadHangs.com assesses equipment</a>. For chalk, grips and accessories, read the <a href="/equipment-guide/">dead hang equipment guide</a>.</p><h2>Which type of pull-up bar fits your space?</h2><p>The right pull-up bar for dead hangs depends on what your space allows. A doorway bar needs a suitable frame, a wall-mounted bar needs studs, a ceiling-mounted bar needs joists and a power tower needs floor space. Pick the mounting type first, then check the exact product.</p><p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Pull-up bar types compared" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Mounting</th><th scope="col">Fit and clearance</th><th scope="col">Load rating to check</th><th scope="col">Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="/doorway-bars/">Doorway</a></td><td>Leverage or pressure against the door frame, no drilling</td><td>Check the opening width and trim depth. Hanging room depends on the door height</td><td>300 pounds or more for bodyweight hangs. No added weight</td><td>Renters and first bars</td></tr><tr><td><a href="/wall-mounted-bars/">Wall-mounted</a></td><td>Lag bolts into wall studs</td><td>Check stud spacing, depth from the wall and room to step down</td><td>400 pounds or more if you plan to add weight</td><td>Owners, long holds and weighted hangs</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling-mounted</td><td>Bolts into overhead joists</td><td>Check where joists sit and the ceiling height. High ceilings may need extension brackets</td><td>400 pounds or more</td><td>Full clearance on every side</td></tr><tr><td>Power tower (freestanding)</td><td>Floor base, assembly only</td><td>Check the footprint and overall height</td><td>Manufacturer's stated rating</td><td>Apartments and spaces where drilling is not possible</td></tr><tr><td>Outdoor or playground</td><td>Anchored in ground or concrete</td><td>Check bar height and condition before use</td><td>Not listed. Inspect for rust and looseness</td><td>Free, no-install hangs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=door+frame+pull+up+bar&tag=deadhangs-20" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer">Browse door frame pull-up bars on Amazon (affiliate link)</a> or <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=wall+mounted+pull+up+bar&tag=deadhangs-20" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener noreferrer">wall-mounted pull-up bars on Amazon (affiliate link)</a>. Compare each listing's stated measurements and rated load against your space.</p><h2>How do you choose a pull-up bar for dead hangs?</h2><p>Choose a dead hang bar by working through seven checks in order: mounting type, measurements, rated load, diameter, finish, instructions and a first-use test. Each step rules out bars that will not work in your space.</p><ol><li><strong>Pick the mounting type</strong> from the table that your space and lease allow.</li><li><strong>Measure the space:</strong> door opening width and trim depth, stud spacing, joist position or floor footprint, plus room above the bar and space to bend your knees.</li><li><strong>Find the rated load</strong> in the product documentation. Use 300 pounds or more for bodyweight hangs and 400 pounds or more for added weight, and stay well under the maximum.</li><li><strong>Check the bar diameter:</strong> about 28 to 32 mm (1.1 to 1.25 inches) lets most hands wrap fully around the bar.</li><li><strong>Check the finish:</strong> powder coat or light knurling grips better than bare chrome.</li><li><strong>Read the mounting instructions</strong> for permitted exercises and the exact fixings or frame requirements.</li><li><strong>Test the mount after fitting:</strong> pull down firmly with both hands before your feet leave the floor, and re-check before each session.</li></ol><h2>What makes a good dead hang bar?</h2>
        <p>A good dead hang bar is stable under a steady load, about 28 to 32 mm across, rated well above your bodyweight and mounted in a way your space allows. A dead hang holds the load continuously, so a loose mount or flexing tube is noticeable.</p>
        <p>A pull-up lasts a few seconds per rep. A hang holds the load continuously. Every weakness in the mounting, frame or grip surface shows up during a long static hold.</p>

        <h3>Stability</h3>
        <p>Zero flex under load is the goal. Wobble or bar rotation makes your forearms stabilize the bar instead of just gripping it. Wall-mounted and ceiling-mounted bars are the most stable because they bolt directly to structural framing. Doorframe bars rank below wall and ceiling bars because they rely on pressure or leverage against the door opening.</p>

        <h3>Bar Diameter</h3>
        <p>Most pull-up bars are 28 to 32 mm (about 1.1 to 1.25 inches) across. Most hands can wrap fully around a bar in that range. Thicker bars are harder to hold. Very thin bars can dig into the palms under load.</p>

        <h3>Weight Capacity</h3>
        <p>Choose a bar rated for at least 300 pounds for bodyweight training. Aim for 400 pounds or more if you add a dip belt for <a href="/weighted-hang-guide/">weighted dead hangs</a>. Stepping off a stool or jumping to the bar briefly loads it more than your bodyweight. A common rule of thumb is to count any added load and stay well under the rated maximum user weight.</p>

        <h3>Mounting Type</h3>
        <p>Your living situation sets the mounting type. Renters usually need a doorframe bar. Homeowners can use the full range of options. Garage gym owners can go straight to wall-mounted or ceiling-mounted bars.</p>

        <div class="cta-box">
